{"title":"axelarator","url":"https://blog.axelarator.net/","favicon":"https://blog.axelarator.net/content/images/size/w256h256/format/jpeg/2025/10/EW7A8077.jpg","elements":[{"title":"We Have Packet Capture at Home","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/we-have-packet-capture-at-home/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2026/05/DSCF2139.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2026/05/DSCF2139.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2026/05/DSCF2139.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2026/05/DSCF2139.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2026/05/DSCF2139.jpg\" alt=\"We Have Packet Capture at Home\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/we-have-packet-capture-at-home/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    We Have Packet Capture at Home\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">When hunting for suspicious activity in an environment, surface level host:port values may only tell so much. For example, Falcon has an #event_simpleName called NetworkConnectIP4. In this event are useful fields like Local/RemoteAddressIP4, Local/RemotePort, and Protocol. Correlating that activity with ProcessRollup2 events could reveal a process</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2026-05-13\">13 May 2026</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">13 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/we-have-packet-capture-at-home/","retrieved":1778630400000},{"title":"Learn By Doing - AWS","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/learn-by-doing-aws/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2026/02/EW7A7610.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2026/02/EW7A7610.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2026/02/EW7A7610.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2026/02/EW7A7610.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2026/02/EW7A7610.jpg\" alt=\"Learn By Doing - AWS\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/learn-by-doing-aws/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Learn By Doing - AWS\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">It's 2026 and I have somehow managed to only work with on-prem environments my entire career thus far. This year though, I plan to change that. Back in December 2025, I decided to start fresh and enrolled in the AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner pathway via https://skillbuilder.aws/</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2026-02-12\">12 Feb 2026</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">12 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/learn-by-doing-aws/","retrieved":1770854400000},{"title":"Cobalt Strike Beacon Analysis","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/cobalt-strike-beacon-analysis/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/12/DSCF0587.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/12/DSCF0587.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/12/DSCF0587.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/12/DSCF0587.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/12/DSCF0587.jpg\" alt=\"Cobalt Strike Beacon Analysis\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/cobalt-strike-beacon-analysis/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Cobalt Strike Beacon Analysis\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">I've written before about how servers with open directories can make it easy to deploy malware but in this post I'll explore a benefit of a single open directory on a site, https://beaconbeagle.com/, which hosts configuration files of Cobalt Strike beacons.\n\nThanks to some</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2025-12-29\">29 Dec 2025</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">9 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/cobalt-strike-beacon-analysis/","retrieved":1766966400000},{"title":"Revisiting a Malicious Word Doc","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/revisiting-a-malicious-word-doc/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/11/000885690013.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/11/000885690013.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/11/000885690013.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/11/000885690013.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/11/000885690013.jpg\" alt=\"Revisiting a Malicious Word Doc\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/revisiting-a-malicious-word-doc/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Revisiting a Malicious Word Doc\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">This research was originally conducted in 2022. An eternity I know. When looking at my notes preparing to add them to this blog, they were lacking a lot of context and in-depth details of what the analysis was revealing. I also hadn't investigated an OLE file in a</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2025-11-17\">17 Nov 2025</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">7 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/revisiting-a-malicious-word-doc/","retrieved":1763337600000},{"title":"Close Those Ports: Exploring Splashtop RMM and Relays","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/close-those-ports-exploring-splashtop-rmm-and-relays/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/11/DSCF1991.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/11/DSCF1991.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/11/DSCF1991.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/11/DSCF1991.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/11/DSCF1991.jpg\" alt=\"Close Those Ports: Exploring Splashtop RMM and Relays\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/close-those-ports-exploring-splashtop-rmm-and-relays/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Close Those Ports: Exploring Splashtop RMM and Relays\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">When looking for suspicious network connectivity, it's important to understand what other services might be running on the destination IP. It could be a harmless Python server with an open directory hosting files but additional analysis revealed it's running Empire at the same time. That open</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2025-11-06\">06 Nov 2025</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">8 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/close-those-ports-exploring-splashtop-rmm-and-relays/","retrieved":1762387200000},{"title":"Hunting for EDR-Freeze","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/hunting-for-edr-freeze/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF1323-2.JPG 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1323-2.JPG 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF1323-2.JPG 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF1323-2.JPG 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1323-2.JPG\" alt=\"Hunting for EDR-Freeze\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/hunting-for-edr-freeze/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Hunting for EDR-Freeze\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">EDR bypassing has become a technique of choice for threat actors and has enabled a market of tools being sold on cybercrime forums:\n\n * Unit42 uncovered a variant of EDRSandBlast being sold on XSS and Exploit.\n * CheckPoint Research uncovered the use of a vulnerable driver, Truesight.sys, which evaded the Microsoft</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2025-10-31\">31 Oct 2025</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">10 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/hunting-for-edr-freeze/","retrieved":1761868800000},{"title":"Homelabs Don't End, They Improve","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/homelabs-dont-end-they-improve/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/000660220008.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/000660220008.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/000660220008.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/000660220008.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/000660220008.jpg\" alt=\"Homelabs Don't End, They Improve\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/homelabs-dont-end-they-improve/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Homelabs Don't End, They Improve\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">There's a never ending list of homelab projects, ways to build one, hardware requirements, goals for running one, single server or 24U rack, the list goes on.\n\nI started my journey long ago when I thought VirtualBox and an external HDD was all I needed. Fast forward to</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2025-10-29\">29 Oct 2025</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">8 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/homelabs-dont-end-they-improve/","retrieved":1761696000000},{"title":"LNK Stomping","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/lnk-stomping/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF1916.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1916.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF1916.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF1916.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1916.jpg\" alt=\"LNK Stomping\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/lnk-stomping/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    LNK Stomping\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">This&nbsp;PoC provided by Elastic&nbsp;is about LNK Stomping. Currently Microsoft has not provided a CVE for this method; however, they did release CVE-2024-38212, a MotW bypass vulnerability, but only included SmartScreen, not Smart App Control (SAC). As this testing is done on Windows 10 with build number 19045, I</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2024-09-04\">04 Sep 2024</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">6 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/lnk-stomping/","retrieved":1725408000000},{"title":"The A in CTI Stands for Actionable","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/the-a-in-cti-stands-for-actionable/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF1404.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1404.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF1404.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF1404.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF1404.jpg\" alt=\"The A in CTI Stands for Actionable\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/the-a-in-cti-stands-for-actionable/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    The A in CTI Stands for Actionable\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">Cyber Threat Intelligence is about communicating the latest information on threat actors and incidents to organizations in a timely manner. Analysis in these areas allows an organization to maintain situational awareness of the current threat landscape, organizational impacts, and threat actor motives. The level of information that needs to be</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2024-04-13\">13 Apr 2024</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">7 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/the-a-in-cti-stands-for-actionable/","retrieved":1712966400000},{"title":"Hunting C2s with Nuclei","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/hunting-c2s-with-nuclei/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF0311.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0311.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF0311.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF0311.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0311.jpg\" alt=\"Hunting C2s with Nuclei\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/hunting-c2s-with-nuclei/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Hunting C2s with Nuclei\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">Overview\n\nFor a long time now, I’ve been using Censys/Shodan and DomainTools to look up hosts, attempt to correlate infrastructure to find overlaps and potentially attribute to C2s and other malicious hosts. There are so many data points to look at like JARM signatures, certificate data including historical</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2023-09-04\">04 Sep 2023</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">10 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/hunting-c2s-with-nuclei/","retrieved":1693785600000},{"title":"Exploring the Mozi Botnet Network","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/exploring-the-mozi-botnet-network/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF0378-1.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0378-1.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF0378-1.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF0378-1.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0378-1.jpg\" alt=\"Exploring the Mozi Botnet Network\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/exploring-the-mozi-botnet-network/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Exploring the Mozi Botnet Network\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">Discovered in 2019, Mozi is a P2P botnet using the DHT protocol that spreads via Telnet with weak passwords and known exploits. Evolved from the source code of several known malware families; Gafgyt, Mirai and IoT Reaper, Mozi is capable of DDoS attacks, data exfiltration and command or payload execution.</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2023-04-11\">11 Apr 2023</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">7 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/exploring-the-mozi-botnet-network/","retrieved":1681171200000},{"title":"Abusing Code Signing Certificates","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/abusing-code-signing-certificates/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/IMG_8442.jpeg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_8442.jpeg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/IMG_8442.jpeg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/IMG_8442.jpeg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_8442.jpeg\" alt=\"Abusing Code Signing Certificates\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/abusing-code-signing-certificates/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Abusing Code Signing Certificates\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">Authenticode Signature\n\nThe point of code signing certificates is to verify the file came from a trusted source, the file was not tampered with prior to receiving it, and the file’s origin can be validated. Code signing creates a hash of the code and encrypts it with a private</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2023-02-15\">15 Feb 2023</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">11 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/abusing-code-signing-certificates/","retrieved":1676419200000},{"title":"Identify the cloud perimeter of a target","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/identify-the-cloud-perimeter-of-a-target/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/IMG_5488-Edit-1.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_5488-Edit-1.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/IMG_5488-Edit-1.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/IMG_5488-Edit-1.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_5488-Edit-1.jpg\" alt=\"Identify the cloud perimeter of a target\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/identify-the-cloud-perimeter-of-a-target/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Identify the cloud perimeter of a target\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">This is a quick guide to\n\n\nIdentify Service\n\n * Use OSINT to determine the provider and region your target is located in.\n   * Shodan for example has a&nbsp;cloud.region&nbsp;filter that lists what region the IP is located in. Some examples:\n     * GCP: us-central1\n     * Azure: northeurope\n     * AWS: us-east-1\n * Download corresponding IP ranges</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2022-08-03\">03 Aug 2022</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">1 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/identify-the-cloud-perimeter-of-a-target/","retrieved":1659484800000},{"title":"Using a Honeypot to Capture TeamTNT Activity","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/using-a-honeypot-to-capture-teamtnt-activity/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/DSCF0936.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0936.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/DSCF0936.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/DSCF0936.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/DSCF0936.jpg\" alt=\"Using a Honeypot to Capture TeamTNT Activity\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/using-a-honeypot-to-capture-teamtnt-activity/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Using a Honeypot to Capture TeamTNT Activity\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">This idea came from a combination of an Intezer guide on building a honeypot and a Sysdig article on triaging malicious Docker containers. I decided to test it myself and stood up an EC2 instance running Ubuntu Server 18.04 w/ Docker running an Alpine Linux container. The actual telemetry</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2022-06-23\">23 Jun 2022</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">4 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/using-a-honeypot-to-capture-teamtnt-activity/","retrieved":1655942400000},{"title":"Qakbot Distributes MSI Files","contents":"<a class=\"post-card-image-link\" href=\"/qakbot-distributes-msi-files/\">\n\n        <img class=\"post-card-image\" srcset=\"/content/images/size/w300/2025/10/IMG_8486.jpg 300w,\n                    /content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_8486.jpg 600w,\n                    /content/images/size/w1000/2025/10/IMG_8486.jpg 1000w,\n                    /content/images/size/w2000/2025/10/IMG_8486.jpg 2000w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 1000px) 400px, 800px\" src=\"/content/images/size/w600/2025/10/IMG_8486.jpg\" alt=\"Qakbot Distributes MSI Files\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\n\n    </a>\n\n    <div class=\"post-card-content\">\n\n        <a class=\"post-card-content-link\" href=\"/qakbot-distributes-msi-files/\">\n            <header class=\"post-card-header\">\n                <div class=\"post-card-tags\">\n                </div>\n                <h2 class=\"post-card-title\">\n                    Qakbot Distributes MSI Files\n                </h2>\n            </header>\n                <div class=\"post-card-excerpt\">Executive Summary\n\n * In mid-April 2022, Mandiant observed UNC2500 campaigns using MSI packages to distribute Qakbot payloads.\n * This change comes shortly after Microsoft’s announcement that macros from Office documents downloaded from the internet (ZoneIdentifier ADS) will be blocked by default.\n * This new payload uses a botnet ID&nbsp;AA,&nbsp;which is</div>\n        </a>\n\n        <footer class=\"post-card-meta\">\n            <time class=\"post-card-meta-date\" datetime=\"2022-06-21\">21 Jun 2022</time>\n                <span class=\"post-card-meta-length\">6 min read</span>\n        </footer>\n\n    </div>","link":"https://blog.axelarator.net/qakbot-distributes-msi-files/","retrieved":1655769600000}]}